WEBVTT

NOTE
Draft captions for the Cucuto explainer video (docs/explainer-video.md). They follow the script's timeline;
adjust the times to the final edit and keep the cue IDs (s<shot>-<what>), shared with explainer.es.vtt.

s1-ping
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.900
[The same plain ping, three times]

s1-vo
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.900
Same sound every time. Who was that?

s2-call
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.200
[Cuckoo call: cu-cu]

s2-vo
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.000
Cucuto gives each WhatsApp
contact a voice.

s3-vo
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:13.600
Set it up once on a rooted
Android 17 phone with LSPosed.

s3-chime
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.000
[Success chime]

s4-vo
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.800
Import a sound pack. The free demo
packs bring eight characters.

s4-chime
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.000
[Success chime]

s5-vo
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.600
Everyone who messaged you shows up here.
Let's give Ada a voice.

s6-vo-hear
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.700
Tap a character to hear it.

s6-cat
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:28.900
[Cat mews]

s6-kettle
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.000
[Kettle whistles]

s6-frog
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:30.900
[Frog ribbits]

s6-vo-assign
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.500
Tap it again, and it's Ada's.

s6-assign
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.400
[Cuckoo call, then the frog ribbits]

s7-door
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:37.400
[Cuckoo call, the frog croaks]

s7-vo
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.000
Next time Ada writes,
you hear Bleep the frog.

s8-vo
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.000
Simulate a message to hear
exactly what's coming.

s8-clip
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.500
[Frog ribbits, the phone vibrates twice]

s9-ada
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.200
[WhatsApp message from Ada:
the frog ribbits]

s9-bea
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:50.500
[Message from Bea: the kettle whistles]

s9-vo
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.800
Now you know who wrote,
without looking.

s10-call
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.000
[Cuckoo call: cu-cu]

s10-vo
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.600
Cucuto. Free and open source,
at cucuto.mauri.app.
